CONTEXT
This repo contains our application codebase plus a folder jobs-logs-error/ holding
production failure logs for AutoSys batch jobs. It has one sub-folder per job
(e.g. jobs-logs-error/<JOB_NAME>/), each containing log files in mixed formats:
.err, .log, .gz (gzipped), possibly rotated logs (.1.log, .log.1), and some may be large.
Look at the real folders and a few real files before you design anything.

GOAL
Build ONE reusable Copilot custom agent, "kb-builder", that produces a comprehensive,
evidence-based knowledge base for one batch job at a time. A future AI support agent
will rely on this knowledge base to diagnose job failures, so accuracy and traceability
matter more than volume.

The agent's inputs:
  1. job name (required), e.g. FIN_EOD_LOAD
  2. path to the job's script in this repo (required), e.g. scripts/batch/fin_eod_load.sh
  3. logs folder (optional, default jobs-logs-error/<job name>/)

Create everything below, then test as described at the end.

======================================================================
PART 1: scripts/prepare_logs.py (log preprocessor, Python standard library only)
======================================================================
Usage: python scripts/prepare_logs.py <logs_folder> [--job <JOB_NAME>]
Needed because Copilot can't read .gz, large logs overflow context, and sensitive data
must be masked before any AI reads it.
1. Read every log file in the folder recursively, including .gz (gzip) and rotated
   logs; open with errors="replace". Skip binaries.
2. Mask, in every line BEFORE writing output: passwords/tokens/secrets/api keys
   (key=value or key: value), credentials inside JDBC/DB/URL connection strings,
   emails, 13-16 digit card-like numbers, ###-##-#### IDs, IP addresses.
3. Detect error lines: ERROR, FATAL, SEVERE, WARN followed by failure, Exception,
   Traceback, "Caused by", ORA-xxxxx, SQLSTATE, DB2 SQLxxxxN, "failed", "cannot",
   "could not", "not found", "no such file", "permission denied", timeouts, "killed",
   out of memory, no space left, non-zero exit/return codes.
4. Group identical errors across files into signatures: replace timestamps, hex,
   file paths, quoted values and plain numbers with placeholders, but KEEP error codes
   (ORA-00054, SQL0911N, HTTP 503) intact. Ids S01, S02..., ranked by number of files.
5. Write work/<JOB>/digest.md:
   - file table: name, size, lines, first/last timestamp, error-line count, signature ids
   - per signature: normalised text, files and counts, up to 2 masked excerpts with
     4 lines of context either side
   - per file: its first error line and its last 15 lines
6. Write work/<JOB>/signatures.csv: id, signature, occurrences, files_seen_in, file_names.
7. Print a one-line summary. Keep digest.md small enough for an AI to read in one go
   (cap excerpts if needed).

======================================================================
PART 2: .github/agents/kb-builder.agent.md (the custom agent)
======================================================================
YAML front matter with a description. The instructions must make the agent do this:

STEP 0: Inputs and preparation
- Confirm the job name, script path and logs folder exist. If anything is missing, ask
  and stop.
- If work/<JOB>/digest.md is missing or older than the logs, run (or ask the user to run)
  python scripts/prepare_logs.py <logs_folder> --job <JOB>.
- Read the existing knowledge/ files for this job and the shared patterns, so it UPDATES
  instead of duplicating.

STEP 1: Understand the code (read-only; never modify code)
Start at the given script and follow what it calls: other scripts, Python/Java/SQL files,
stored procedures, config/properties files and environment variables. Follow at most
3 levels deep; list anything it couldn't follow. Extract:
- purpose of the job in plain English
- entry point and step-by-step flow
- inputs: files (paths/patterns), DB tables/views read, APIs, upstream data
- outputs: files written, tables inserted/updated/deleted/truncated, messages sent
- external systems: databases, SFTP/file shares, APIs, queues
- configuration and parameters (names only, NEVER secret values)
- every place the code logs an error, raises an exception or exits non-zero:
  message text, exit code, file:line, the condition that triggers it
- retries, timeouts, commits/rollbacks, cleanup on failure
- rerun safety: does a rerun duplicate data (plain inserts/appends) or is it safe
  (truncate-and-reload, merge/upsert, idempotency checks)? Cite the code.

STEP 2: Understand the failures
Read work/<JOB>/digest.md and signatures.csv. Reading rules:
- the FIRST real error in a file is usually the root cause; later lines are consequences
- generic lines ("Exiting with code 1", "job failed") are symptoms, never causes
- in stack traces the innermost "Caused by" or the DB/OS error code is usually the cause
- the same signature id across files means the same failure recurring

STEP 3: Connect logs to code
For each signature, find where in the code that message or exit code is produced,
or where the failing operation happens (e.g. the SQL statement behind an ORA error).
Explain what the code was doing at that moment and what conditions could cause it.
This is the most valuable part of the knowledge base.

STEP 4: Write the knowledge base
  knowledge/README.md
     How the knowledge base is organised and how a future agent should use it:
     look up the error in error-catalog.csv, then read the pattern, then the job files.
     Create it once and keep it current.
  knowledge/index.md
     Catalogue of all jobs and patterns, one line each, with links.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/overview.md
     Purpose, script path, schedule/box (if known, else "unknown – ask"), inputs,
     outputs, upstream/downstream dependencies, external systems, SLA, owner.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/code-map.md
     Flow of the job step by step with file:line references, key functions, config
     keys, plus an "error and exit-code map" table: message/exit code | file:line |
     trigger condition | meaning.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/failure-history.md
     One row per failed run (log file): date/time, first error, signature, pattern,
     what the code was doing.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/troubleshooting.md
     A runbook per failure type seen: how to recognise it, what to check to confirm
     (concrete commands or queries where the code makes them obvious), likely fix,
     what NOT to do, when to escalate.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/rerun-safety.md
     Verdict yes/no/depends with code evidence, the risks (duplicates, partial loads,
     downstream re-triggers) and preconditions for a safe rerun.
  knowledge/patterns/P###-<short-name>.md
     One per distinct failure type, shared across jobs (reuse and extend a matching
     pattern; add this job to its list). Sections: Status, Signature (exact text, error
     codes), Jobs seen in (with counts), Usual cause, How to confirm, Fix, Do NOT, Safe to
     rerun, Escalate to, Seen (count, date range, example log files).
  knowledge/error-catalog.csv
     Machine-readable lookup for the future agent, one row per signature per job:
     signature_id, job, normalised_error, error_code, source_location(file:line),
     pattern_id, category (data|dependency|infrastructure|database|code|credentials|
     unknown), confidence (high|medium|low), status.
  data/failures.csv
     One row per log file: failure_id, date_time, job_name, log_file, exit_code,
     first_error, error_code, signature_id, pattern_id, root_cause, fix_applied,
     safe_to_rerun, ticket, status. Leave root_cause, fix_applied, safe_to_rerun and
     ticket EMPTY unless logs or code prove them. status = draft.
  knowledge/jobs/<JOB>/open-questions.md
     Numbered questions for the production support team: per pattern, confirm the cause,
     the fix used, rerun safety, escalation team; plus schedule/SLA/owner gaps, code paths
     the agent couldn't follow, and unclassified logs.

STEP 5: Finish
Print a summary: logs processed, signatures found, patterns created/updated, code files
read, open questions raised, and anything it couldn't determine.

STRICT RULES for the agent (put these in the agent file):
- Every claim carries its evidence: [LOG: file] or [CODE: path:line]. Anything inferred
  is marked "DRAFT – to confirm with production support".
- Quote error text exactly. Never invent log lines, jobs, tables, times or file paths.
- Read-only on code. Never copy secrets from config into the knowledge base; record
  config key NAMES only. Never unmask [MASKED]/[EMAIL]/[IP] values.
- Never state "safe to rerun: yes" without code evidence; even then it stays DRAFT
  until production support confirms.
- Prefer "unknown" over a guess. Every file gets "Status: draft" until a human confirms.
- Update existing files rather than overwriting human-confirmed content; anything
  marked "confirmed" is only changed by appending notes.

